the tear gas gave the clue that  something was happening  a large group of people was approaching  the border with seota from the moroccan  side  we watched and waited and soon saw heads  bobbing in the water  the first group of around 20 swimming  their way into spanish territory  on the shore soldiers were waiting the  military drafted in after thousands made  this journey in the last few days  the swimmers were told to come ashore  and emerge  shivering and cold after their time in  the scene  all were male almost all young the fact  seemingly not lost on the military who  would soon take them to a local facility  but many have made it through this week  you see them everywhere close to the  border  new inhabitants of the streets of saota  we met these teenagers who evaded the  authorities  and told us of their journey to get here  this boy shows us the cuts he got on the  rocks  saying they were in the sea for an hour  another says they spotted the police but  weren't caught  thirty-year-old kareem from southern  morocco made the same journey  he didn't tell his family he was leaving  at this center the red cross is trying  to provide basic support to some of the  more than eight  thousand people who've made it to ceyuta  this week  including more than fifteen hundred  children and teenagers  they're given food and water and tested  for covid their future's uncertain  and like the many adults who've already  been returned to morocco  this spanish territory in north africa  is struggling to cope with the numbers  leaving many residents frustrated  i'm from morocco and i know my country  although i do live here in siota  people coming here are not looking for  jobs  it's a problem there's not the  infrastructure here to shelter so many  illegal migrants  foreign many locals are furious at  reports morocco relaxed its border  controls  following a diplomatic spat with spain  prompting this week's influx  but on this side of the border they're  taking an increasingly hard line  ceyuta has long been a focus for  migrants and asylum seekers from africa  trying to make it to europe  but the number getting in this week is  unprecedented spain has taken a hard  line already sending  thousands back and spain's border  problem is also  the european union's but from the  evidence we saw the presence of the  police and military is proving little  deterrent so far  and the worry is more people will try to  come over the summer months  michelle clifford sky news seyuta
 

